  "verbalization": "Salmeterol, a member of the adrenergic beta2-agonists class, is linked to Adrb2 through a series of molecular interactions. The evidence suggests that Salmeterol, as part of this class, binds to Adrb2 through a shared pathway involving the adrenergic system. This is supported by the fact that Salmeterol and its analogs, such as Pirbuterol and Formoterol, all bind to Adrb2, indicating a common mechanism of action. The lowest common ancestor of Salmeterol and its analogs is the adrenergic beta2-agonist class, which in turn includes compounds that interact with Adrb2. The presence of Orciprenaline, a member of this class, further supports the notion that Salmeterol's interaction with Adrb2 is mediated through a broader adrenergic signaling pathway. The integration of these pathways highlights the role of adrenergic receptors in the pharmacological effects of Salmeterol, with Adrb2 being a key target in this context.",
